\section{Reproducibility and caching}{

By default (\code{seed = NA}) the random seed is derived from the data and the
parameters. A plot therefore looks the same every time it is drawn, and
several layers of the same data share one embedding. Use an integer seed
to choose another layout, or \code{NULL} for a new random layout on every draw.

Results are cached in memory (see \code{\link[=umap_cache_clear]{umap_cache_clear()}}), so printing,
resizing or saving a plot does not recompute the embedding.

With \code{flavour = "0.5"} and more than one thread, the optimisation is
parallel and not bit-for-bit reproducible across sessions; use
\code{n_threads = 1} or \code{flavour = "0.6"} if you need exact reproducibility.
}

